WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:08.800 line:84%
EAMMON WEED: My
name is Eammon Weed,

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.280 line:84%
and I am a sophomore currently
studying history and English.

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.420 line:84%
Interestingly enough,
four years ago,

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.810 line:84%
I had the delight of meeting
this afternoon's speaker

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:20.682 line:84%
at the Acton Institute, when I
was a sophomore in high school.

00:00:20.682 --> 00:00:22.390 line:84%
And it was by mere
coincidence that I was

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:24.870 line:90%
asked to introduce him today.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.860 line:84%
And so, it is with
great pleasure

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.350 line:84%
that I introduce our next
speaker, Dr. Glenn Arbery.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.970 line:84%
Dr. Glenn C. Arbery
is the president

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:36.070 line:90%
of Wyoming Catholic College.

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.920 line:84%
He earned his PhD in
literature and politics

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.290 line:90%
from the University of Dallas.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:44.620 line:84%
He has taught at the University
of St. Thomas, Thomas Moore

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.610 line:84%
College of Liberal Arts,
the University of Dallas,

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:51.430 line:84%
and Assumption College,
where he held the d'Alzon

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:54.190 line:90%
Chair of Liberal Education.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.980 line:84%
He has served as Director
of the Teachers Academy

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:00.070 line:84%
at the Dallas Institute
of Humanities and Culture

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.280 line:84%
and as an editor of the
People Newspapers in Dallas,

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.290 line:84%
where he won regional
and national awards

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.180 line:90%
for his writing.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.020 line:84%
In addition to numerous
essays and reviews,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.190 line:84%
Dr. Arbery is the
author or editor

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.840 line:84%
of Why Literature Matters, The
Southern Critics, The Tragic

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.460 line:84%
Abyss, and Augustine's
Confessions and its Influence.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.740 line:84%
Please join me in
welcoming Dr. Arbery.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.662 line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:37.618 --> 00:01:39.410 line:84%
GLENN ARBERY: Eamonn
reminded me last night

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:42.950 line:84%
when I saw him that I tried to
recruit him to Wyoming Catholic

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.800 line:90%
College four years ago.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.200 line:84%
I'm glad now that we
didn't get him, since--

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:49.520 line:90%
that's not a coincidence.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.850 line:84%
How could that be a
coincidence, right?

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.580 line:90%
These things come around.

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:59.690 line:84%
About 25 years ago, Jeff Nelson,
at the Intercollegiate Studies

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:02.660 line:84%
Institute called me
up one day to ask me

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:06.380 line:84%
if I would write a book
about why literature matters.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.800 line:90%
Foolishly, I agreed to do it.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.540 line:84%
And now, every few years
I get an invitation

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:16.940 line:84%
from someone who hopes I have
by now figured out what to say.

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:20.330 line:84%
This time it was my old
friend Doug Jeffrey.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.490 line:84%
When we were at the
University of Dallas,

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:26.840 line:84%
he drove if not the only, at
least the coolest Karmann Ghia

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.030 line:90%
in Dallas County.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.850 line:84%
He persuaded me, during
the blank-eyed lulls

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.340 line:84%
of writing my
dissertation, to read

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.120 line:84%
the novels of Ross
MacDonald, which I remember

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.472 line:90%
with particular gratitude.

00:02:40.472 --> 00:02:44.330 line:84%
I add to it my gratitude for
this invitation to Hillsdale

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:48.490 line:84%
College, which has been
a beacon of independence

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:51.620 line:84%
in American thought,
like the signal flare

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:56.060 line:84%
that Homer describes late in
The Iliad that goes pulsing

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:00.680 line:84%
high for men of the neighboring
islands to see it in case

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:06.080 line:84%
they might come over in ships
to help beat off the enemy.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.960 line:84%
The question, why
literature matters,

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.570 line:84%
comes up now once again in the
context of liberal education.

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:17.330 line:84%
Even before there were
academies of course,

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:21.670 line:84%
poetry formed the minds
of those who heard it.

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:24.050 line:84%
And literature has
been, from the beginning

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.660 line:84%
of the Western
tradition, a central way

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:31.460 line:84%
of educating the young and
enlightening their elders.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.730 line:84%
But at least since
Plato, it has also

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:37.550 line:84%
been suspect
because of its power

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:42.800 line:84%
in forming opinion without the
authority of careful argument.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.440 line:84%
So what is this
continuing appeal?

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.120 line:84%
It's not merely entertainment,
though it unquestionably

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:52.140 line:90%
gives pleasure.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.170 line:84%
Even now, literature
is a common language

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:58.560 line:84%
for those who are
educated, and an ability

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:02.400 line:84%
to move gracefully among
literary references

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:08.550 line:84%
to poems, novels, and plays,
adds to beauty of character.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:12.300 line:84%
Why literature matters
today, however,

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:15.150 line:84%
becomes a particularly
pressing question

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:18.690 line:84%
when we think about the
graceless controversies that

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:22.200 line:84%
surround it, when it
is treated primarily

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.830 line:90%
as an ideological tool.

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:31.710 line:84%
Back in December, a
piece about #DisruptTexts

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:35.370 line:84%
by Meghan Cox Gurdon of
the Wall Street Journal

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:40.230 line:84%
caused a stir when it exposed
the dismissal of The Odyssey

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:43.200 line:84%
by a high school
in Massachusetts.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.390 line:84%
Very proud to say
we got The Odyssey

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:50.040 line:84%
removed from the curriculum
this year, one teacher declared.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.440 line:84%
The teachers in
Massachusetts apparently

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:57.690 line:84%
believed that the longer ago
the now banished Odyssey was

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:01.770 line:84%
written, the more
objectionable it was.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:06.210 line:84%
They must have imagined an
outbreak of hurtful cyclops

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:07.248 line:90%
jokes.

00:05:07.248 --> 00:05:09.000 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.470 line:84%
Perhaps the harsh judgment of
the marginalized lotus-eaters

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.270 line:90%
offended them.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:18.240 line:84%
In any case, there's
nothing new in their attempt

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.600 line:84%
to get the classics
out of the curriculum.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:25.200 line:84%
The famous, "hey, hey,
ho, ho, Western culture's

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:30.210 line:84%
got to go" protest about the
civilization course at Stanford

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.980 line:90%
occurred in 1987.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:37.350 line:84%
And I remember a
conference at LSU in 1991

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:40.560 line:84%
when a cadre of college
teachers would not

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.070 line:84%
read The Aeneid,
for the seminar,

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:48.630 line:84%
because it would be collusion
in the European colonization

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:51.580 line:90%
of Latin America.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:53.700 line:90%
This is sensitive stuff.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.760 line:84%
So better not read
The Odyssey either.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:00.570 line:84%
According to disrupt
text, young people

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:05.490 line:84%
should read only works written
in the present day vernacular,

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:11.130 line:84%
works that promote
and center voices that

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:17.970 line:84%
are LGBQTIA plus and BIPOC,
Black indigenous and People

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:20.240 line:90%
of Color.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.210 line:84%
There are many
reasonable answers,

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:26.660 line:84%
many reasons why it's important
to keep great literature

00:06:26.660 --> 00:06:31.400 line:84%
in the curriculum, especially
when it requires a challenging

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:35.010 line:84%
encounter with
unfamiliar language.

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:37.940 line:84%
Reading a deeply
engaging work, older

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:41.240 line:84%
by far than the
political structures

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:44.600 line:84%
and racial divisions
of the Western world,

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:49.010 line:84%
one that precedes Socrates,
Plato, and Aristotle

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:52.970 line:84%
by centuries, not to
mention, Rome, Christian

00:06:52.970 --> 00:06:56.420 line:84%
Europe, and the
Americas might begin

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:59.300 line:84%
to overcome what
Alan Tate described

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:02.450 line:90%
as provincialism in time--

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:04.850 line:84%
the belief that this
moment of history

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:07.160 line:90%
is the judge of all others.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:12.850 line:84%
Roger Kimball last night
described it as presentism.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:16.820 line:84%
The sociologist Peter
Berger diagnosed

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:19.870 line:84%
the essential complaint
against the classics,

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:25.280 line:84%
50 years ago, in the 1970
essay called The Obsolescence

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:28.160 line:90%
of the Concept of Honor.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:30.470 line:84%
He'd just
distinguished societies

00:07:30.470 --> 00:07:33.080 line:90%
based on clear roles--

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:36.790 line:84%
for example, the warrior
world of The Iliad

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:39.560 line:84%
from modern ones
based on dignity,

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:42.860 line:84%
understood as
intrinsic humanity,

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:49.460 line:84%
divested of all socially
imposed roles or norms.

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:53.090 line:84%
As Berger explains, looking
back from his perspective

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:57.920 line:84%
on the 1960s, quote,
"The social location

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:01.370 line:84%
of honor in a
world of relatively

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:06.290 line:84%
intact stable institutions, a
world in which individuals can

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:10.850 line:84%
with subjective certainty
attach their identities

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:15.350 line:84%
to the institutional roles
that society assigns to them."

00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:20.330 line:84%
This is what he understands
by the world of honor.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:23.660 line:84%
For example, in
a world of honor,

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:28.580 line:84%
a poet would find writing
an ode or a sonnet part

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:32.000 line:90%
of the role of being a poet.

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.960 line:84%
He or she would find
it an opportunity

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:39.159 line:84%
for demonstrating
individual poetic talent,

00:08:39.159 --> 00:08:43.030 line:84%
in measuring mastery
of a challenging

00:08:43.030 --> 00:08:46.390 line:90%
form against past writers.

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:51.580 line:84%
Even romantic revolutionaries
like Shelley wrote sonnets.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:56.180 line:84%
In societies that reject
honor, on the other hand,

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:58.450 line:90%
institutions- this is Berger--

00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:02.800 line:84%
"Cease to be the
home of the self.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.890 line:84%
Instead they become
oppressive realities that

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:09.130 line:90%
distort and estrange the self.

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:12.510 line:84%
Roles no longer
actualize the self,

00:09:12.510 --> 00:09:16.510 line:84%
but serve as a veil of
Maya, hiding the self

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:19.900 line:84%
not only from others,
but from the individual's

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:21.840 line:90%
own consciousness."

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.470 line:90%
End quote.

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:27.640 line:84%
In other words, the coercive
demands of the sonnet

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:31.270 line:84%
distort or silence
self expression.

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:35.050 line:84%
And the poet should reject
any social role that requires

00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:38.810 line:90%
submission to such forms.

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:42.890 line:84%
So, for contemporary
movements like disrupt text,

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:46.090 line:84%
the honored works of
the Western tradition

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:50.260 line:84%
have become institutional
forms, imposed by those

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:52.360 line:90%
who control the cannon--

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:56.440 line:84%
oppressive realities from
which students must be

00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:02.650 line:84%
liberated in order to find
their own voices and identities.

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:05.680 line:84%
Berger strikes me as
particularly pressing it

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:08.140 line:84%
when he writes that
the individual who

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:11.050 line:84%
rejects the world
of honor can only

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:16.840 line:84%
hope to define himself quote,
"in the interstitial spaces

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:20.290 line:84%
left vacant by
the institutions."

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:25.600 line:84%
For example, as self expression
and gender identity moves

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:30.690 line:84%
away from all socially
imposed roles or norms,

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:32.890 line:84%
it's literature
tries to dissolve

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:36.610 line:84%
any standard of excellence
not based on dignity

00:10:36.610 --> 00:10:40.030 line:90%
or individual self expression.

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:43.060 line:84%
Ironically, what this
means in practice

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:48.760 line:84%
is that writers supposedly
in the cultural interstices

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:52.810 line:84%
win literary prizes and
prestigious university

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:58.540 line:84%
positions that honor dissent
as a new social norm, which

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:02.020 line:84%
makes it hard to describe
these writers as anything

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:05.710 line:84%
but the new
institutional mainstream.

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:08.750 line:90%
One gets confused.

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:12.530 line:84%
But let's avoid the
sirens of grievance.

00:11:12.530 --> 00:11:15.200 line:84%
Two reasons that
literature matters

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.840 line:90%
seem to me most pressing.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:21.410 line:84%
One, is what my teacher at the
University of Dallas, Louise

00:11:21.410 --> 00:11:25.790 line:84%
Cowan, called cosmopoiesis,
or world-making,

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:30.740 line:84%
in her introduction to
the epic cosmos in 1984.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:33.860 line:84%
The second involves
literature as the mode

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:39.650 line:84%
of concrete or embodied thought,
most natural to the human mind.

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:43.940 line:84%
That is, thinking in images,
comparisons, characters,

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:46.660 line:90%
speeches, and actions.

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:49.610 line:84%
The characters and
actions of literature

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:54.040 line:84%
are offered to the common
judgment of mankind.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:57.730 line:84%
The best works endure
because they most wisely

00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:01.000 line:84%
represent human
and divine things

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:05.260 line:84%
and give us the pleasure of
recognizing our lives in more

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:06.220 line:90%
noble contours.

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:12.830 line:84%
Let me start with the
second, literature

00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:15.170 line:90%
as a mode of thought.

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:18.890 line:84%
Every household of
parents and children

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:21.980 line:84%
has a cast of
distinct characters

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:26.510 line:84%
whose various performances
become stories in the family.

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:29.960 line:84%
One of our four-year-old
grandsons for example,

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:34.060 line:84%
though a domestic
terrorist by day,

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:38.450 line:84%
sang a beautiful solo of
Silent Night on Christmas Eve,

00:12:38.450 --> 00:12:41.150 line:90%
much to our astonishment.

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:44.780 line:84%
Every classroom could
provide Ben Johnson

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:47.720 line:84%
with examples of the
humors and so could

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:50.120 line:90%
every day in an office.

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:56.240 line:84%
Anyone engaged in making, excuse
me, in managing other people

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:59.800 line:84%
thinks in characters
and actions.

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:02.050 line:84%
Who's brave enough
to risk the boss'

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:06.320 line:84%
rage over a personal fault
that's hurting the company?

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:10.240 line:84%
What will Grove do to
handle this slight?

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:13.780 line:84%
Did you see how Pritchett
treated that woman?

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:16.180 line:84%
How do you think Clotka
will react when we

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:19.300 line:90%
present this proposal to her?

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:22.660 line:84%
This way of thinking
is so natural to us

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:25.710 line:84%
that we hardly consider
it thought at all.

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:28.180 line:84%
And attempts to
reduce it to a system,

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:31.930 line:84%
say in the scenarios of
business advice books,

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:35.190 line:90%
always seem naive.

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:38.250 line:84%
The philosopher himself,
as Dante calls him,

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:42.570 line:84%
bases his account of virtue
in the Nicomachean Ethics

00:13:42.570 --> 00:13:47.460 line:84%
not on a set of principles, but
on the character and actions

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:51.780 line:84%
of the spoudaios, the
serious admirable man

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:56.790 line:84%
imitated by those who want to
exhibit courage or prudence.

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:01.170 line:84%
Novels, plays, and epics,
work through the depiction

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:02.820 line:90%
of character.

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:06.750 line:84%
At the beginning of The Iliad,
for example, only Achilles

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:10.260 line:84%
will stand up to Agamemnon
when he brings down

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:13.140 line:90%
a plague on the entire army.

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:18.960 line:84%
And only Achilles suffers the
mortal insult of the king.

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:21.780 line:84%
Through his mother, Achilles
asked Zeus for the honor

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:24.570 line:84%
that Agamemnon has denied
him, even though it

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:29.160 line:84%
will mean the deaths of many and
the defeat of his own friends.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:33.820 line:84%
Both men bring harm to their
own army, in other words.

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:38.100 line:84%
But which is more noble,
Agamemnon or Achilles?

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:41.130 line:84%
For anyone who's read
The Iliad, the answer

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:43.800 line:90%
is obviously Achilles.

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:49.170 line:84%
Saying why takes the reader
into the depths of the poem.

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:52.500 line:84%
Later in The Iliad, the
Achaeans have been driven back

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:55.650 line:84%
inside their newly built
wall and the Trojans

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.950 line:90%
occupy the whole plain.

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:03.060 line:84%
Diomedes volunteers to find out
how the Trojans are arranged

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:06.030 line:90%
and what their plans might be.

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:11.000 line:84%
Who does he want with him on
this dangerous night raid?

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:12.420 line:90%
Nestor?

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:14.550 line:90%
Huge Ajax?

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:16.410 line:90%
Menelaus?

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:19.080 line:90%
No, he wants Odysseus.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:21.570 line:90%
And so would you.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:24.060 line:90%
The judgment is instantaneous.

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:25.130 line:90%
Why?

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:27.240 line:90%
He had to tease it out.

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:31.950 line:84%
You'd say that unexpected
situations don't rattle him,

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:35.340 line:84%
that he's quick-witted
and physically powerful,

00:15:35.340 --> 00:15:37.170 line:90%
and that he's a superb liar.

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:45.450 line:84%
We naturally condense what
a person says and does,

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:48.150 line:84%
as well as what other
people say about him,

00:15:48.150 --> 00:15:52.860 line:84%
into a single charged
image of who he or she is.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:54.930 line:90%
It can be wrong.

00:15:54.930 --> 00:15:56.860 line:90%
It often is wrong.

00:15:56.860 --> 00:15:59.370 line:84%
And this possibility
of error also

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:03.790 line:84%
forms part of what
literature shows.

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:08.470 line:84%
King Lear banishes the
daughter who loves him most.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:11.980 line:84%
Othello is so wrong
about Desdemona,

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:15.400 line:84%
thinking that she has shamed
him and his culture of military

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:18.910 line:84%
honor, that he
cannot let her live.

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:23.620 line:84%
But even as he kills her,
he adores her, praises her,

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:26.890 line:84%
tries to save her
from damnation.

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:32.050 line:84%
After he kills her, he discovers
her innocence and cries,

00:16:32.050 --> 00:16:35.380 line:84%
Whip me, you devils,
from the possession

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:37.900 line:90%
of this heavenly sight.

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:41.920 line:84%
Blow me about and winds,
roast me in sulfur.

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:47.200 line:84%
Wash me in steep down
gulfs of liquid fire.

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:50.080 line:84%
Othello becomes for
us a figure embodying

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:54.610 line:84%
many cultural complexities,
a part of our thought.

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:58.780 line:84%
Like him, hundreds of other
characters from literature

00:16:58.780 --> 00:17:02.260 line:84%
become part of the way we
think, as though we knew them

00:17:02.260 --> 00:17:04.250 line:90%
personally--

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:15.050 line:84%
Clytemnestra, Penelope, Dido,
Cordelia, Hester Prynne.

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:19.280 line:84%
Literature helps form
judgment and right feeling.

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:23.300 line:84%
In fact, it helps us
order experience itself.

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:26.060 line:84%
The accuracy of
its depictions may

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:29.300 line:84%
be constantly tested
against people

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:33.180 line:90%
we know, or might come to know.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:35.400 line:84%
As a teacher, it
never occurs to me

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:39.330 line:84%
to try to abstract a
concept from characters

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:45.720 line:84%
like Raskolnikov, Captain
Ahab, or Don Quixote,

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:48.420 line:84%
as though they merely
stood for something

00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:50.580 line:84%
that could be more
tidily handled

00:17:50.580 --> 00:17:53.550 line:90%
on the conceptual level.

00:17:53.550 --> 00:17:57.390 line:84%
In 19th century St.
Petersburg, Raskolnikov

00:17:57.390 --> 00:18:01.470 line:84%
lives in a milieu of
radical social thought.

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:06.810 line:84%
And his mind has been formed
by fashionable theories.

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:10.620 line:84%
But unlike many others,
he feels the compulsion

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:14.640 line:84%
to test himself by putting
these theories into action.

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:16.770 line:84%
There's a kind of
integrity in that,

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:20.220 line:84%
even though it involves
killing an old pawnbroker.

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:23.490 line:84%
But he therefore suffers
the full consequences

00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:25.770 line:90%
of what he does.

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:29.340 line:90%
A student learns Raskolnikov--

00:18:29.340 --> 00:18:33.360 line:84%
not a concept that he stands
for, and not even really

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:36.870 line:84%
the character Raskolnikov,
as though he were merely

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:40.500 line:84%
an exhibit of the
novelist's imagination.

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:44.070 line:84%
Knowing Raskolnikov
pulls the reader

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:48.330 line:84%
into the whole rich, social,
and spiritual context

00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:51.120 line:90%
of Dostoevsky's novel.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:54.690 line:84%
Great characters, like
people we've known deeply

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:59.760 line:84%
in our lives, become complex
attractors that order

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:02.730 line:90%
and deepen daily understanding.

00:19:02.730 --> 00:19:06.180 line:84%
Literature leads us into
thinking more deeply

00:19:06.180 --> 00:19:11.050 line:84%
in and through our
own experience.

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:13.250 line:84%
Literature is a
mode of knowledge.

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:17.260 line:84%
It also involves a deep
pleasure in language itself--

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:21.210 line:84%
this would be a good
time to put up the poem--

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:24.090 line:90%
in the revelation of likenesses.

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:27.000 line:84%
For example, Gerard
Manley Hopkins,

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:30.000 line:84%
in his sonnet, The
Starlight Night,

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.190 line:84%
thinks in a series of
metaphors about the stars.

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:38.370 line:84%
His language is dense
with illiteration,

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:42.360 line:84%
full of heavy stresses that
form a kind of counterweight

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:47.550 line:84%
to the upward vision, and all
of this in the imperative mood.

00:19:47.550 --> 00:19:50.010 line:84%
Imagine a student
used to reading works

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:53.740 line:84%
in the present day vernacular
suddenly buttonholed

00:19:53.740 --> 00:19:55.030 line:90%
by Hopkins' poem.

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:02.540 line:90%
"Look at the stars!

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:05.390 line:90%
Look, look up at the skies!

00:20:05.390 --> 00:20:09.140 line:84%
Oh, look at all the
fire-folk sitting in the air!

00:20:09.140 --> 00:20:13.460 line:84%
The bright boroughs, the
circle-citadels there!

00:20:13.460 --> 00:20:16.820 line:84%
Down in dim woods
the diamond delves!

00:20:16.820 --> 00:20:18.680 line:90%
The elves'-eyes!

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:25.120 line:84%
The grey lawns cold where
gold, where quickgold lies!

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:27.230 line:90%
Wind-beat white beam!

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:30.350 line:90%
Airy abeles set on a flare!

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:35.032 line:84%
Flake-doves sent floating
forth at a farmyard scare!

00:20:35.032 --> 00:20:36.440 line:90%
Ah well!

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:40.070 line:84%
It is all a purchase,
all is a prize.

00:20:40.070 --> 00:20:41.360 line:90%
Buy then!

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:42.830 line:90%
Bid then!

00:20:42.830 --> 00:20:43.570 line:90%
What?

00:20:43.570 --> 00:20:48.140 line:90%
Prayer, patience, alms, vows.

00:20:48.140 --> 00:20:52.850 line:84%
Look, look, a May-mess
like on orchard boughs!

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:53.450 line:90%
Look!

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:58.640 line:84%
March-bloom, like on
mealed-with-yellow sallows!

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:04.170 line:84%
These are indeed the barn;
withindoors house the shocks.

00:21:04.170 --> 00:21:07.370 line:84%
This piece-bright
paling shuts the spouse.

00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:14.800 line:84%
Christ home, Christ and his
mother and all his hollows."

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:18.160 line:84%
Now we could pause over
the word quickgold, which

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:22.850 line:84%
Hopkins invented, according to
the Oxford English Dictionary.

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:25.300 line:90%
Quicksilver we know.

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:28.810 line:84%
It's the older and more
descriptive term for mercury

00:21:28.810 --> 00:21:31.870 line:84%
and it's kind of
jittery liveliness.

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:36.520 line:84%
So we make the transfer and see
the gray lawns cold where gold,

00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:38.980 line:90%
where quickgold lies.

00:21:38.980 --> 00:21:43.060 line:84%
The cold dew drops quick
with the golden sunlight

00:21:43.060 --> 00:21:45.070 line:90%
on the lawns.

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:49.960 line:84%
The stars become the features
of this kind of English estate.

00:21:49.960 --> 00:21:54.220 line:84%
They flare like white beam
leaves tossed by the wind.

00:21:54.220 --> 00:22:00.480 line:84%
They burst into sight like
doves scared up from a farmyard.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:04.140 line:90%
And this estate is for sale.

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:08.400 line:84%
Hopkins tells us,
buy then, bid then.

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:09.540 line:90%
Buy with what?

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:13.080 line:84%
With prayer,
patience, alms, vows.

00:22:13.080 --> 00:22:14.940 line:90%
Again, he commands us to look.

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:23.490 line:84%
In the last metaphorical
transformation,

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:26.490 line:84%
the whole sky of
the starlight night

00:22:26.490 --> 00:22:29.330 line:84%
becomes a single structure
if you follow what

00:22:29.330 --> 00:22:32.760 line:90%
he's doing in this last part.

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:36.450 line:84%
These are indeed the barn,
that is the whole night

00:22:36.450 --> 00:22:38.060 line:90%
sky you're looking at.

00:22:38.060 --> 00:22:42.660 line:84%
Is to be conceived
as the single barn.

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:46.170 line:84%
And inside the barn
are the sheaves

00:22:46.170 --> 00:22:51.240 line:84%
stored up after the harvest,
the gathered saints, let's say.

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:55.290 line:84%
Remember the old song,
Bringing in the Sheaves?

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:56.530 line:90%
But these are also--

00:22:56.530 --> 00:22:57.810 line:90%
notice the enjambment.

00:22:57.810 --> 00:23:00.930 line:84%
Notice the way that the
shocks is left there

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:04.230 line:84%
at the beginning of that
next to the last line.

00:23:04.230 --> 00:23:11.070 line:84%
The greatest shocks are those of
the unexpected, surpassed hope

00:23:11.070 --> 00:23:13.140 line:90%
or understanding.

00:23:13.140 --> 00:23:16.950 line:84%
The greatest shock of
all is the last image

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:21.150 line:84%
of immense interior
light, pouring out

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:27.090 line:84%
through the tiny gaps and holes
in the palings of the house,

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:31.230 line:84%
of this barn that houses
Christ and his mother in all

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:35.700 line:84%
his hollows, as though the
whole starry sky were simply

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:41.070 line:84%
a vision of that light breaking
through in these tiny places.

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:43.950 line:84%
Why this repeated demand to
look as though we had never

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:46.230 line:90%
seen the skies?

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:49.350 line:84%
The Russian critic,
Viktor Shklovsky

00:23:49.350 --> 00:23:51.690 line:84%
wrote a century
ago that perception

00:23:51.690 --> 00:23:56.970 line:84%
can become so habitual that
it becomes algebraization.

00:23:56.970 --> 00:24:01.500 line:84%
Wordsworth complains, little
we see in nature that is ours.

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:06.000 line:84%
But Hopkins shocks us, out
of laying waste our powers.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:07.290 line:90%
Look at the stars.

00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:09.960 line:90%
Look, look up at the skies.

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:12.150 line:84%
He also shocks us
from the way that we

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:16.080 line:84%
see dew or wind blown
trees or the flowers

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:18.690 line:90%
on spring orchard boughs.

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:21.300 line:84%
Thinking in
metaphors, we discover

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:25.870 line:84%
the beauty of familiar things
put next to each other.

00:24:25.870 --> 00:24:29.070 line:84%
As Hopkins says
in another sonnet,

00:24:29.070 --> 00:24:34.750 line:84%
beauty keeps warm men's
wits to the things that are.

00:24:34.750 --> 00:24:39.360 line:84%
Now, this in itself is
already an accomplishment.

00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:43.500 line:84%
By the late 19th century
when Hopkins is writing,

00:24:43.500 --> 00:24:45.780 line:84%
time and space
and divine purpose

00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:49.620 line:84%
had all suffered major
shocks from modern science.

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:53.940 line:84%
The deep time of geology,
the increasing vastness

00:24:53.940 --> 00:24:57.780 line:84%
of the discoverable universe,
the apparently random

00:24:57.780 --> 00:25:00.450 line:84%
adaptation of creatures
to environments

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:05.260 line:84%
in Darwin's theories of
evolution, and in general,

00:25:05.260 --> 00:25:08.550 line:84%
a de-centering of man
and the human intellect,

00:25:08.550 --> 00:25:14.970 line:84%
and a disenchanting of the world
outside the Cartesian subject.

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:18.390 line:84%
So what Hopkins is
doing here is a bid

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:22.470 line:84%
toward that other dimension of
literature that I think we see,

00:25:22.470 --> 00:25:27.990 line:84%
and what Louise Cowan calls
world-making, or cosmopoiesis.

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:32.820 line:84%
This cosmos, this world
that the poet makes,

00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:36.480 line:84%
as Louise Cowan says,
"establishes the mundus

00:25:36.480 --> 00:25:42.000 line:84%
imaginalis wherein reside
the universal qualities that

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:44.670 line:90%
make up the human condition.

00:25:44.670 --> 00:25:47.850 line:84%
It's the glory of
the poetic cosmos

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:52.350 line:84%
to show these verities in
action, embedded in matter,

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:54.210 line:84%
bearing the weight
and expressing

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:58.320 line:84%
the hope of the finite
order," end quote.

00:25:58.320 --> 00:26:02.350 line:84%
A poetic cosmos is
a whole apprehended

00:26:02.350 --> 00:26:07.500 line:84%
by the imagination, everywhere
charged with meaning--

00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:11.020 line:84%
a lived world of sky
and earth; the living

00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:16.120 line:84%
and the dead, cities and
households, gods and mortals.

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:19.120 line:84%
It's an interpretive
order, a felt world

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:25.750 line:84%
that gives a place to the whole
realm of human experience.

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:28.510 line:84%
World-making doesn't
mean rhetorically

00:26:28.510 --> 00:26:32.260 line:84%
affirming the structures and
institutions that are already

00:26:32.260 --> 00:26:35.650 line:84%
there so much as it
means subjecting them

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:37.660 line:90%
to poetic revision and scrutiny.

00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:44.560 line:84%
For example, think what
Homer does in both The Iliad

00:26:44.560 --> 00:26:47.310 line:90%
and The Odyssey.

00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:50.340 line:84%
Agamemnon's outrageous
insult to Achilles

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:54.270 line:84%
calls into question the
justice of awarding or denying

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:57.150 line:84%
honors when the standards
of the one in charge

00:26:57.150 --> 00:27:00.390 line:84%
are personal or
politically biased.

00:27:00.390 --> 00:27:05.160 line:84%
Suppose that someone's greatness
exceeds the city's capacity

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:09.650 line:84%
to contain it in its
system of social roles

00:27:09.650 --> 00:27:12.610 line:90%
and conventional honors.

00:27:12.610 --> 00:27:16.870 line:84%
Does such greatness destroy
the city or transfigure it?

00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:19.980 line:84%
That's the question
that The Iliad poses.

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:24.970 line:84%
In The Odyssey, marriage
undergoes a major reimagining.

00:27:24.970 --> 00:27:27.770 line:84%
The reunion of
Odysseus and Penelope

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:30.430 line:84%
doesn't restore
something normal.

00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:32.950 line:84%
I think we tend to
think that, that

00:27:32.950 --> 00:27:36.200 line:84%
here was a marriage of
Penelope and Odysseus--

00:27:36.200 --> 00:27:39.860 line:84%
Odysseus gets back home and
everything's back to normal.

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:43.390 line:84%
But the reunion of
Odysseus and Penelope

00:27:43.390 --> 00:27:47.260 line:84%
is by no means restoring
something normal.

00:27:47.260 --> 00:27:50.980 line:84%
Their marriage is establishing
a startling, unheard

00:27:50.980 --> 00:27:56.780 line:84%
of ideal of fidelity
and mutual mindedness.

00:27:56.780 --> 00:28:00.640 line:84%
Whoever had a marriage
like this man?

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:02.650 line:84%
When Jacques Maritain
writes that poetry

00:28:02.650 --> 00:28:05.500 line:84%
is an inner communication
between the inner being

00:28:05.500 --> 00:28:09.280 line:84%
of things and the inner
being of the human self,

00:28:09.280 --> 00:28:12.670 line:84%
I think we have to take
"things" to include not only

00:28:12.670 --> 00:28:17.910 line:84%
natural ones, but
also institutions.

00:28:17.910 --> 00:28:19.570 line:84%
I could say more
about this, but I

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:23.890 line:84%
want to move on to the
idea of what a cosmos looks

00:28:23.890 --> 00:28:26.860 line:84%
like by looking at
the ancient world,

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:32.260 line:84%
and particularly about looking
at The Iliad and The Odyssey

00:28:32.260 --> 00:28:36.040 line:84%
next to the modern
cosmos that I think

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:41.760 line:84%
we feel sort of atom-braided
in Hopkins' poem.

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:46.290 line:84%
Divine presence is everywhere
in the ancient world.

00:28:46.290 --> 00:28:50.460 line:84%
In The Iliad and The Odyssey,
Zeus the cloud-gatherer

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:55.980 line:84%
rules the sky and unpredictable
Poseidon, the sea.

00:28:55.980 --> 00:29:00.870 line:84%
Bleak Hades governs the realm
of the shades in the underworld.

00:29:00.870 --> 00:29:04.320 line:84%
And far below them lie
the halls of Tartarus,

00:29:04.320 --> 00:29:09.660 line:84%
where the ancient defeated gods
lie in their eternal bondage.

00:29:09.660 --> 00:29:13.750 line:84%
So, what is the good,
disrupt text might say,

00:29:13.750 --> 00:29:18.360 line:84%
of offering young readers
an outmoded cosmos of gods

00:29:18.360 --> 00:29:21.180 line:84%
nobody believes in,
instead of showing them

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:25.430 line:84%
their own social circumstances
in the vernacular.

00:29:25.430 --> 00:29:28.260 line:84%
Again, there are too
many things to say.

00:29:28.260 --> 00:29:34.130 line:84%
But perhaps the most important,
is that entering an epic cosmos

00:29:34.130 --> 00:29:37.700 line:84%
keeps cultural identity
and historical circumstance

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:39.770 line:90%
from being a prison.

00:29:39.770 --> 00:29:41.750 line:84%
If there is a
barrier of disbelief,

00:29:41.750 --> 00:29:43.680 line:90%
it's easily overcome.

00:29:43.680 --> 00:29:47.090 line:84%
And this poetic cosmos
is not closed to us

00:29:47.090 --> 00:29:51.380 line:84%
because we inhabit it with the
cues of imagination natural

00:29:51.380 --> 00:29:54.440 line:90%
to us from our own reality.

00:29:54.440 --> 00:29:57.260 line:90%
One scene from The Iliad--

00:29:57.260 --> 00:30:00.290 line:84%
there's a line in my book,
Why Literature Matters,

00:30:00.290 --> 00:30:01.230 line:90%
which ends--

00:30:01.230 --> 00:30:05.150 line:84%
well, the whole point of it is
that God loves The Iliad more

00:30:05.150 --> 00:30:06.920 line:90%
than any other poem.

00:30:06.920 --> 00:30:09.620 line:84%
This is why I keep
coming back to this.

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:13.010 line:84%
One scene from The Iliad
will help make my point here.

00:30:13.010 --> 00:30:16.130 line:84%
At the end of book
eight, the consequences

00:30:16.130 --> 00:30:18.620 line:84%
of Achilles' withdrawal
from the fighting

00:30:18.620 --> 00:30:21.320 line:90%
have become unmistakable.

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:25.610 line:84%
Hector has just succeeded in
driving the terrified Achaeans

00:30:25.610 --> 00:30:31.130 line:84%
back inside the wall that was
hastily constructed to protect

00:30:31.130 --> 00:30:33.830 line:90%
the ships and the shelters.

00:30:33.830 --> 00:30:36.470 line:84%
For the first time in
the war, The Achaeans

00:30:36.470 --> 00:30:38.180 line:90%
are on the defensive.

00:30:38.180 --> 00:30:43.300 line:84%
And Hector exalts in his
unprecedented success

00:30:43.300 --> 00:30:45.470 line:84%
as the Trojans stay
out on the plain

00:30:45.470 --> 00:30:50.030 line:84%
to prevent the Achaeans from
escaping in their ships.

00:30:50.030 --> 00:30:53.840 line:84%
They expect to end the long
war by the next day, which

00:30:53.840 --> 00:30:58.160 line:84%
Agamemnon on the other side had
thought earlier in the poem.

00:30:58.160 --> 00:31:00.440 line:84%
In this mood, the
citizens bring out

00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:04.670 line:84%
from the city oxen, and
fat sheep in all speed,

00:31:04.670 --> 00:31:10.310 line:84%
and also the kindly sweet wine
with food out of their houses,

00:31:10.310 --> 00:31:14.900 line:84%
while the fighting men heap
up many piles of firewood.

00:31:14.900 --> 00:31:17.720 line:84%
For all the grimness
of war, this scene

00:31:17.720 --> 00:31:20.660 line:90%
feels almost carnivalesque.

00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:23.180 line:90%
The city is wide open.

00:31:23.180 --> 00:31:25.940 line:84%
And the soldiers are
enjoying this respite

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:29.400 line:84%
from their years trapped
inside the walls.

00:31:29.400 --> 00:31:34.550 line:84%
Homer ends this book with a
poem, with a complex simile.

00:31:34.550 --> 00:31:36.310 line:90%
Whoops.

00:31:36.310 --> 00:31:38.840 line:90%
There we go.

00:31:38.840 --> 00:31:42.940 line:84%
"So with hearts made
high these sat night-long

00:31:42.940 --> 00:31:46.510 line:84%
by the outworks of battle,
and their watchfires

00:31:46.510 --> 00:31:49.690 line:90%
blazed numerous about them.

00:31:49.690 --> 00:31:53.020 line:84%
As when in the sky the stars
about the moon's shining

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:55.720 line:84%
are seen in all
their glory, when

00:31:55.720 --> 00:32:00.100 line:84%
the air has fallen to stillness,
and all the high places

00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:04.590 line:84%
of the hills are clear, and
the shoulders out-jutting,

00:32:04.590 --> 00:32:07.780 line:84%
and the deep ravines,
as endless bright air

00:32:07.780 --> 00:32:10.740 line:90%
spills from the heavens

00:32:10.740 --> 00:32:13.390 line:84%
And all the stars are
seen, to make glad

00:32:13.390 --> 00:32:17.290 line:84%
the heart of this shepherd;
such in their numbers blazed

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:20.170 line:84%
the watchfires the
Trojans were burning

00:32:20.170 --> 00:32:25.600 line:84%
between the waters of Xanthos
and the ships, before Ilion.

00:32:25.600 --> 00:32:28.450 line:84%
A thousand fires were
burning in the plain,

00:32:28.450 --> 00:32:32.620 line:84%
and beside each one sat
fifty men in the glare

00:32:32.620 --> 00:32:35.170 line:90%
of the blazing firelight.

00:32:35.170 --> 00:32:39.610 line:84%
And standing each beside his
chariot, champing white barley

00:32:39.610 --> 00:32:43.660 line:84%
and oats, the horses
waited for the dawn

00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:46.390 line:90%
to mount to her high place."

00:32:46.390 --> 00:32:47.215 line:90%
I love this simile.

00:32:50.420 --> 00:32:54.980 line:90%
So, what's going on in it?

00:32:54.980 --> 00:32:58.370 line:84%
One thing that's
going on in it is

00:32:58.370 --> 00:33:00.860 line:84%
that there's a movement
of the imagination away

00:33:00.860 --> 00:33:04.160 line:84%
from the scene of
the fires far away,

00:33:04.160 --> 00:33:08.710 line:84%
to the shepherd in the hills
looking up at the stars.

00:33:08.710 --> 00:33:13.490 line:84%
I'm reminded, when I read this,
of our students at Wyoming

00:33:13.490 --> 00:33:15.510 line:84%
Catholic who go out
on a winter trip.

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:17.960 line:84%
In fact, they just
went out last week.

00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:19.970 line:90%
And they go up into the Tetons.

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:22.430 line:84%
And at night they do
cross-country skiing

00:33:22.430 --> 00:33:29.390 line:84%
and build their ice
houses and see these stars

00:33:29.390 --> 00:33:31.980 line:84%
with this unpolluted
sky that this shepherd,

00:33:31.980 --> 00:33:36.320 line:84%
3,000 years ago in Homer's
simile, would have seen.

00:33:38.870 --> 00:33:41.110 line:84%
Then you come back down
to the fires, which

00:33:41.110 --> 00:33:47.510 line:84%
have been immortalized in this
kind of vision of the heavens.

00:33:47.510 --> 00:33:50.540 line:84%
Then you come back to each
fire, with the light and warmth

00:33:50.540 --> 00:33:51.980 line:90%
of that fire.

00:33:51.980 --> 00:33:54.680 line:84%
And then almost like
a flash of a camera

00:33:54.680 --> 00:33:59.180 line:84%
for a second, the faces of
those 50 men around each fire.

00:33:59.180 --> 00:34:01.680 line:84%
And then the last
movement of the image

00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:06.190 line:84%
to those horses
leaning over, almost

00:34:06.190 --> 00:34:12.400 line:84%
as though the white barley were
a kind of meal of stars leaning

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:14.030 line:90%
down to the earth.

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:18.520 line:84%
Gaston Bachelard writes
that a particular cosmos

00:34:18.520 --> 00:34:21.010 line:84%
forms around a
particular image, as soon

00:34:21.010 --> 00:34:26.600 line:84%
as the poet gives the image
the destiny of grandeur.

00:34:26.600 --> 00:34:28.840 line:84%
The grandeur of
Homer's image pulls us

00:34:28.840 --> 00:34:34.630 line:84%
directly into the cosmos of the
poem and its unfolding drama.

00:34:34.630 --> 00:34:38.530 line:84%
These Trojans would not
be spending the night

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:44.500 line:84%
outside the city had Zeus not
nodded his head to Achilles'

00:34:44.500 --> 00:34:46.960 line:84%
mother Thetis at
the end of book one

00:34:46.960 --> 00:34:49.900 line:84%
and promised to
give Achilles honor.

00:34:49.900 --> 00:34:52.989 line:84%
By the end of book
eight, the absence

00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:57.310 line:84%
of Achilles from the fighting
has drawn these Trojans out

00:34:57.310 --> 00:35:01.240 line:84%
onto the plain, full
of a false confidence.

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:04.450 line:84%
Under Hector, they
enjoy this night

00:35:04.450 --> 00:35:08.350 line:84%
with hearts made high
only because they do not

00:35:08.350 --> 00:35:15.410 line:84%
imagine the return of
the warrior Achilles.

00:35:15.410 --> 00:35:21.650 line:84%
The sense of vastness, the
starry sky, the nearby ocean,

00:35:21.650 --> 00:35:23.750 line:90%
the walled city--

00:35:23.750 --> 00:35:28.940 line:84%
all these give space to the
unfolding destiny of Achilles,

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:31.520 line:84%
the hero deprived
at his conception

00:35:31.520 --> 00:35:34.460 line:84%
of supreme immortality
and now being

00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:37.550 line:90%
given his poetic compensation.

00:35:37.550 --> 00:35:41.720 line:84%
Embedded in this moment
of Trojans celebration

00:35:41.720 --> 00:35:47.000 line:84%
are already the fires of
the destruction of Troy.

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:51.050 line:84%
These households bringing
out their food and sweet wine

00:35:51.050 --> 00:35:56.400 line:84%
will very soon be ransacked
in the passions of war.

00:35:56.400 --> 00:35:59.150 line:84%
But for a moment,
Homer transcends

00:35:59.150 --> 00:36:02.540 line:84%
the dramatic circumstance
of war in a scene

00:36:02.540 --> 00:36:04.760 line:90%
of clarity and beauty.

00:36:04.760 --> 00:36:09.530 line:84%
And all those such moments, in
retrospect, reveal more deeply

00:36:09.530 --> 00:36:12.680 line:84%
and poignantly the
unfolding tragedy.

00:36:12.680 --> 00:36:15.890 line:84%
They also remain
in the imagination

00:36:15.890 --> 00:36:20.300 line:84%
as a promise of a more enduring
peace and a truer homeland.

00:36:22.940 --> 00:36:27.020 line:84%
The peculiarity of the
imagination is nobility,

00:36:27.020 --> 00:36:30.540 line:84%
Wallace Stevens wrote
during World War II.

00:36:30.540 --> 00:36:35.270 line:84%
In this inherent nobility is
a natural source of another,

00:36:35.270 --> 00:36:39.110 line:84%
which our extremely
headstrong generation

00:36:39.110 --> 00:36:42.260 line:90%
regards as false and decadent.

00:36:42.260 --> 00:36:44.810 line:84%
"I mean," Stevens
says, "that nobility,

00:36:44.810 --> 00:36:50.610 line:84%
which is our spiritual
height and depth," end quote.

00:36:50.610 --> 00:36:53.570 line:84%
There's nothing more
conspicuously absent

00:36:53.570 --> 00:36:56.300 line:84%
from the literature
of our own day

00:36:56.300 --> 00:37:01.320 line:84%
than the world-making
grandeur of the imagination.

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:06.110 line:84%
We feel the urgency of
getting past hypersensitive

00:37:06.110 --> 00:37:11.060 line:84%
intersectional grievances
and trigger warnings.

00:37:11.060 --> 00:37:15.490 line:84%
But how do we begin
the ennobling recovery?

00:37:15.490 --> 00:37:19.770 line:84%
As Yeats says of the soul,
in Sailing to Byzantium,

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:24.330 line:84%
it needs to study monuments
of its own magnificence,

00:37:24.330 --> 00:37:27.990 line:84%
these world-making
works that give us

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:31.380 line:84%
the great characters and
actions, the enduring

00:37:31.380 --> 00:37:33.330 line:90%
metaphors.

00:37:33.330 --> 00:37:37.650 line:84%
But precisely those
are being Rejected.

00:37:37.650 --> 00:37:42.330 line:84%
So suppose we take
our cue from Achilles.

00:37:42.330 --> 00:37:44.670 line:84%
Instead of letting
our great literature

00:37:44.670 --> 00:37:51.830 line:84%
be insulted and counseled,
we deliberately withdraw it.

00:37:51.830 --> 00:37:55.790 line:84%
We deny Homer and
Shakespeare, say,

00:37:55.790 --> 00:37:59.120 line:84%
to those below a
certain income level

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:02.060 line:84%
or those who identify
themselves as belonging

00:38:02.060 --> 00:38:04.960 line:90%
to certain social groups.

00:38:04.960 --> 00:38:08.020 line:84%
And we greatly
encourage letting them

00:38:08.020 --> 00:38:11.680 line:84%
read only things that flatter
their own current level

00:38:11.680 --> 00:38:13.180 line:90%
of knowledge.

00:38:13.180 --> 00:38:17.690 line:84%
And implicitly we forbid them
to aspire to anything else.

00:38:20.750 --> 00:38:21.710 line:90%
This is outrageous.

00:38:21.710 --> 00:38:23.300 line:90%
[SCOFFS]

00:38:23.300 --> 00:38:25.850 line:84%
But I am hard
pressed to say what

00:38:25.850 --> 00:38:29.315 line:84%
the difference is between
this program and disrupt text.

00:38:32.070 --> 00:38:37.200 line:84%
It's a pleasure to meet someone
denied the classics in college,

00:38:37.200 --> 00:38:40.100 line:84%
who comes upon
them later in life,

00:38:40.100 --> 00:38:42.990 line:84%
and can hardly believe the
extent of their earlier

00:38:42.990 --> 00:38:44.970 line:90%
deprivation.

00:38:44.970 --> 00:38:49.440 line:84%
At places like Hillsdale and
Wyoming Catholic College,

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:52.770 line:84%
we study the great tradition
of literature in which

00:38:52.770 --> 00:38:56.130 line:90%
wisdom adds to wisdom--

00:38:56.130 --> 00:39:01.140 line:84%
The Bible, The Odyssey, The
Aeneid, The Divine Comedy,

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:04.470 line:84%
Paradise Lost, the great
plays of Shakespeare

00:39:04.470 --> 00:39:08.100 line:84%
and others, the great
novels, the great lyrics.

00:39:08.100 --> 00:39:12.980 line:84%
We hold on to the past
because we hold on to hope.

00:39:12.980 --> 00:39:15.930 line:84%
Stevens ends his
essay, The Noble Rider

00:39:15.930 --> 00:39:20.100 line:84%
and the Sound of Words, with
a simile both of consolation

00:39:20.100 --> 00:39:22.110 line:90%
and challenge.

00:39:22.110 --> 00:39:27.420 line:84%
As a wave is a force and not the
water of which it is composed,

00:39:27.420 --> 00:39:31.080 line:84%
which is never the
same, so nobility

00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:38.760 line:84%
is a force, and not
the manifestations

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:43.290 line:84%
of which it is composed,
which are never the same.

00:39:43.290 --> 00:39:49.420 line:84%
Nobility of spirit exists
now, as it has always existed.

00:39:49.420 --> 00:39:52.530 line:84%
And more than ever, we
need a deeper knowledge

00:39:52.530 --> 00:39:54.560 line:90%
of our great literature.

00:39:54.560 --> 00:39:56.030 line:90%
Why?

00:39:56.030 --> 00:40:00.050 line:84%
To help us cultivate an
honor in our students,

00:40:00.050 --> 00:40:03.980 line:84%
the imagination to
renew our institutions

00:40:03.980 --> 00:40:07.830 line:84%
and make a new world
from the best of the old.

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:08.330 line:90%
Thank you.

00:40:08.330 --> 00:40:11.294 line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:18.220 --> 00:40:19.900 line:84%
EAMMON WEED: Thank
you, Dr. Arbery.

00:40:19.900 --> 00:40:22.930 line:84%
We now have time for some
Q&amp;A. If you have a question,

00:40:22.930 --> 00:40:24.610 line:84%
please make your way
down to the front.

00:40:43.490 --> 00:40:45.050 line:84%
AUDIENCE: Hi, thank
you for the talk.

00:40:45.050 --> 00:40:47.800 line:84%
I was wondering, do you
think that the prevalence

00:40:47.800 --> 00:40:51.850 line:84%
of superhero characters
in modern storytelling

00:40:51.850 --> 00:40:54.535 line:84%
is a result of the
deprivation of epic heroes?

00:40:57.147 --> 00:40:58.730 line:84%
GLENN ARBERY: That's
a great question.

00:40:58.730 --> 00:41:03.220 line:84%
In fact, when I was thinking
about the absence of grandeur

00:41:03.220 --> 00:41:05.440 line:84%
in literature, I'm
really thinking

00:41:05.440 --> 00:41:11.470 line:84%
more about sort of mainstream
novels and plays and so on.

00:41:11.470 --> 00:41:13.600 line:84%
While in the
popular imagination,

00:41:13.600 --> 00:41:17.020 line:84%
I guess we can call it,
there's a teaming kind

00:41:17.020 --> 00:41:21.670 line:84%
of world of superheroes and
semi-gods and so on that's

00:41:21.670 --> 00:41:22.780 line:90%
going on.

00:41:22.780 --> 00:41:25.990 line:90%
And these have huge popularity.

00:41:25.990 --> 00:41:29.140 line:84%
So even when you're
trying to get

00:41:29.140 --> 00:41:32.140 line:84%
across the nature
of the Homeric gods,

00:41:32.140 --> 00:41:35.450 line:84%
the temptation is
always to say--

00:41:35.450 --> 00:41:38.530 line:84%
OK, what's the difference
between, say, Captain America

00:41:38.530 --> 00:41:42.550 line:90%
and Iron Man or something?

00:41:42.550 --> 00:41:46.420 line:84%
What's the difference
between the kind of mutation

00:41:46.420 --> 00:41:49.150 line:84%
that leads to some
kind of superpowers

00:41:49.150 --> 00:41:52.750 line:84%
and mere technological
advantage?

00:41:52.750 --> 00:41:55.990 line:84%
But I think the impulse
is there toward some kind

00:41:55.990 --> 00:42:01.420 line:84%
of heightening, some kind
of middle way of imagining

00:42:01.420 --> 00:42:08.260 line:84%
between ordinary people and the
sort of inaccessible divine.

00:42:08.260 --> 00:42:10.240 line:84%
So yeah, I think you're
onto something there.

00:42:10.240 --> 00:42:10.740 line:90%
Thank you.

00:42:13.990 --> 00:42:15.880 line:84%
AUDIENCE: Thank
you for your talk.

00:42:15.880 --> 00:42:17.860 line:84%
As someone who,
and I'm sure there

00:42:17.860 --> 00:42:19.510 line:84%
are other people in
the room like this,

00:42:19.510 --> 00:42:21.628 line:84%
planning on teaching
in the future--

00:42:21.628 --> 00:42:24.170 line:84%
anything from elementary school
all the way up to high school

00:42:24.170 --> 00:42:25.600 line:90%
and possibly beyond--

00:42:25.600 --> 00:42:30.070 line:84%
I wanted to ask for
either a piece of advice

00:42:30.070 --> 00:42:32.920 line:84%
or how you yourself
have inhabited

00:42:32.920 --> 00:42:36.430 line:84%
these aspects of world-making
in concrete and embodied

00:42:36.430 --> 00:42:40.010 line:84%
thought in your own teaching
and in your own classes.

00:42:40.010 --> 00:42:42.130 line:90%
GLENN ARBERY: Thank you.

00:42:42.130 --> 00:42:44.230 line:84%
I think the most
important thing to do

00:42:44.230 --> 00:42:46.690 line:84%
when you're teaching
literature, and I

00:42:46.690 --> 00:42:50.410 line:84%
have to say I learned this
most of all from Louise Cowan,

00:42:50.410 --> 00:42:52.660 line:90%
is to make the students--

00:42:52.660 --> 00:42:56.960 line:84%
or to help the student imagine
what's going on in the story.

00:42:56.960 --> 00:43:04.990 line:84%
In other words, if you're always
going at a poem or a novel

00:43:04.990 --> 00:43:09.940 line:84%
in order to extract from it what
seems to you the salient idea,

00:43:09.940 --> 00:43:12.700 line:84%
you're not treating
it as literature.

00:43:12.700 --> 00:43:18.850 line:84%
You're treating it as a
kind of candy wrapper.

00:43:18.850 --> 00:43:22.610 line:84%
And down inside
is the real thing.

00:43:22.610 --> 00:43:25.630 line:84%
There are always
depths to anything

00:43:25.630 --> 00:43:28.010 line:90%
that's important in literature.

00:43:28.010 --> 00:43:31.090 line:84%
But the first thing
to do is to make

00:43:31.090 --> 00:43:33.430 line:84%
that effort of
imagination, which takes

00:43:33.430 --> 00:43:35.630 line:90%
you deeply into the story.

00:43:35.630 --> 00:43:39.220 line:84%
I remember Louise Cowan
teaching Moby-Dick when

00:43:39.220 --> 00:43:41.470 line:90%
I was a graduate assistant.

00:43:41.470 --> 00:43:47.320 line:84%
And she was treating a novel
which I had read before

00:43:47.320 --> 00:43:48.910 line:90%
but didn't like.

00:43:48.910 --> 00:43:52.960 line:84%
And suddenly, reading into
those scenes of the novel

00:43:52.960 --> 00:43:56.020 line:84%
when Ishmael and
Queequeg and Starbuck

00:43:56.020 --> 00:43:59.080 line:84%
are chasing this
huge pod of whales,

00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:01.900 line:84%
and suddenly they get
drawn into the center--

00:44:01.900 --> 00:44:05.260 line:84%
this kind of still center
where all the whales

00:44:05.260 --> 00:44:06.460 line:90%
are circling around.

00:44:06.460 --> 00:44:08.380 line:84%
And they can see
down into the depths

00:44:08.380 --> 00:44:12.910 line:84%
of the sea at the young
leviathans and their mothers.

00:44:12.910 --> 00:44:15.370 line:84%
I mean, it's just
marvelous, as soon

00:44:15.370 --> 00:44:18.250 line:84%
as you get the image,
instead of simply

00:44:18.250 --> 00:44:22.100 line:84%
concentrating on what seemed
to you the conceptual part.

00:44:22.100 --> 00:44:26.220 line:84%
I think this is true of children
as it is of college students.

00:44:26.220 --> 00:44:32.200 line:84%
That is, this is the first
thing you do, imagine

00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:35.410 line:84%
into the nature of
the world that's being

00:44:35.410 --> 00:44:37.820 line:90%
given in that work.

00:44:37.820 --> 00:44:43.870 line:84%
What's it like to inhabit
a body in that world?

00:44:43.870 --> 00:44:48.140 line:84%
I think that's important
to always think about.

00:44:48.140 --> 00:44:50.350 line:84%
In other words, when
you're reading something,

00:44:50.350 --> 00:44:52.390 line:90%
you enter it in a body.

00:44:52.390 --> 00:44:55.690 line:84%
You have a kind of fictive
body or imaginal body.

00:44:55.690 --> 00:44:57.880 line:84%
And, to the extent that
the writer is good,

00:44:57.880 --> 00:45:01.820 line:84%
you find yourself grounded
in at home in that world.

00:45:01.820 --> 00:45:06.767 line:84%
So I think that's a first
way to go with that.

00:45:06.767 --> 00:45:07.850 line:90%
Thank you, great question.

00:45:10.537 --> 00:45:12.870 line:84%
AUDIENCE: Thank you so much
for coming in to talk to us.

00:45:12.870 --> 00:45:14.780 line:84%
So being classically
educated, we

00:45:14.780 --> 00:45:16.700 line:84%
have the opportunity
to read these books

00:45:16.700 --> 00:45:18.650 line:90%
and to be helped through them.

00:45:18.650 --> 00:45:21.830 line:84%
But sometimes, because of
that, some of my favorite books

00:45:21.830 --> 00:45:22.460 line:90%
are classics.

00:45:22.460 --> 00:45:25.670 line:84%
But how do you do it when
you're no longer in school

00:45:25.670 --> 00:45:28.340 line:84%
and you're no longer being
taught these classics?

00:45:28.340 --> 00:45:29.120 line:90%
Are you doomed?

00:45:29.120 --> 00:45:31.460 line:84%
Is it once you're out of
college and are no longer

00:45:31.460 --> 00:45:33.620 line:84%
being taught the classics
you can't enjoy them?

00:45:33.620 --> 00:45:35.720 line:84%
How do you enjoy
these classic books?

00:45:35.720 --> 00:45:36.350 line:84%
GLENN ARBERY: (SARCASTICALLY)
Well, you don't.

00:45:36.350 --> 00:45:38.060 line:90%
You just go watch The Avengers.

00:45:38.060 --> 00:45:39.455 line:90%
You know?

00:45:39.455 --> 00:45:40.850 line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:45:40.850 --> 00:45:44.780 line:84%
No, I think having
people who also

00:45:44.780 --> 00:45:46.710 line:84%
like to read them
is pretty important.

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:51.770 line:84%
So communities of readers, I
think, are vital to anything

00:45:51.770 --> 00:45:56.580 line:84%
that you're really going to
be able to continue to do.

00:45:56.580 --> 00:46:00.090 line:84%
It's great to read these
things on your own.

00:46:00.090 --> 00:46:03.140 line:84%
It's just, as you
say, more difficult.

00:46:03.140 --> 00:46:06.920 line:84%
I think it's easier
to read novels

00:46:06.920 --> 00:46:09.650 line:84%
than it is to read,
say, the epic poems.

00:46:09.650 --> 00:46:13.970 line:84%
But still, I think you always
long for the conversation that

00:46:13.970 --> 00:46:16.190 line:90%
goes on about these things.

00:46:16.190 --> 00:46:22.730 line:84%
Just as you talk about bosses
and colleagues and friends,

00:46:22.730 --> 00:46:26.330 line:84%
you want to talk about
the characters and actions

00:46:26.330 --> 00:46:31.370 line:84%
that you read about in this
literature, because of the way

00:46:31.370 --> 00:46:34.280 line:84%
it illuminates the
things around you.

00:46:34.280 --> 00:46:36.650 line:84%
It gives you a
different perspective

00:46:36.650 --> 00:46:39.380 line:90%
on what might have gotten stale.

00:46:39.380 --> 00:46:43.680 line:84%
If you start seeing your
friend as Clytemnestra,

00:46:43.680 --> 00:46:45.290 line:90%
you're worried.

00:46:45.290 --> 00:46:54.980 line:84%
If you start seeing
Penelope in your mother,

00:46:54.980 --> 00:46:57.950 line:84%
you know, your mother as
striking you as Penelope--

00:46:57.950 --> 00:46:59.510 line:90%
tell your dad to go home.

00:46:59.510 --> 00:47:02.030 line:90%
All right, you know.

00:47:02.030 --> 00:47:03.200 line:90%
It's been 20 years.

00:47:03.200 --> 00:47:05.070 line:90%
Go home, Dad.

00:47:05.070 --> 00:47:05.600 line:90%
I'm sorry.

00:47:09.660 --> 00:47:10.160 line:90%
Thank you.

00:47:12.208 --> 00:47:13.750 line:84%
AUDIENCE: Thank you
for coming today.

00:47:16.310 --> 00:47:20.500 line:84%
So obviously when we read
these traditional books,

00:47:20.500 --> 00:47:24.800 line:84%
and the epics especially,
we're learning

00:47:24.800 --> 00:47:29.660 line:84%
from the experiences of
timeless experiences really--

00:47:29.660 --> 00:47:34.880 line:84%
and as you said, wisdom
building on wisdom.

00:47:34.880 --> 00:47:40.160 line:84%
And more modern literature,
like you were talking about,

00:47:40.160 --> 00:47:44.660 line:84%
it's somewhat blind
to that wisdom.

00:47:44.660 --> 00:47:48.560 line:84%
So it's hard to build upon
that when it isn't there

00:47:48.560 --> 00:47:49.770 line:90%
in the first place.

00:47:49.770 --> 00:47:56.930 line:84%
So how important is it for
us to maybe expose ourselves

00:47:56.930 --> 00:47:58.820 line:84%
to the more modern
literature, when

00:47:58.820 --> 00:48:03.290 line:84%
they might make the argument
as well that only reading

00:48:03.290 --> 00:48:09.020 line:84%
traditional books flatters
our own ideas, like we can say

00:48:09.020 --> 00:48:12.210 line:90%
sometimes about the other side?

00:48:12.210 --> 00:48:13.880 line:84%
GLENN ARBERY:
Excellent question.

00:48:13.880 --> 00:48:16.165 line:84%
I think we should
read everything.

00:48:16.165 --> 00:48:21.320 line:84%
I read modern novels just
as I read the classics.

00:48:21.320 --> 00:48:24.230 line:84%
In fact, you're
looking all the time,

00:48:24.230 --> 00:48:25.880 line:84%
I think, for the
work that's going

00:48:25.880 --> 00:48:29.900 line:84%
to be a breakthrough, something
that's going to really address

00:48:29.900 --> 00:48:36.410 line:84%
us in a way that is world-making
in the sense I'm speaking of.

00:48:36.410 --> 00:48:41.060 line:84%
We need writers who
aren't just trying

00:48:41.060 --> 00:48:44.630 line:84%
to undercut everything that's
good about the tradition,

00:48:44.630 --> 00:48:48.800 line:84%
but really try to remake
it to see how to take it

00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:52.400 line:84%
into a different
social circumstance,

00:48:52.400 --> 00:48:54.950 line:90%
political circumstance.

00:48:54.950 --> 00:49:01.550 line:84%
What do you do in a world
where not everybody believes

00:49:01.550 --> 00:49:06.170 line:84%
in God in the same way
that Dante's world did?

00:49:06.170 --> 00:49:08.540 line:84%
How do you establish
that relation

00:49:08.540 --> 00:49:10.490 line:84%
between the human
and the divine that's

00:49:10.490 --> 00:49:14.720 line:84%
so crucial to understanding
what it feels like to be

00:49:14.720 --> 00:49:16.370 line:90%
in the Homeric world?

00:49:16.370 --> 00:49:17.090 line:90%
Right?

00:49:17.090 --> 00:49:22.500 line:90%
Where are we finding that done?

00:49:22.500 --> 00:49:27.930 line:84%
So I think reading modern
literature is always

00:49:27.930 --> 00:49:31.070 line:84%
a kind of urging
of modern writers

00:49:31.070 --> 00:49:33.410 line:84%
to take up this
challenge and to really

00:49:33.410 --> 00:49:39.170 line:84%
try to see what that would
be like if you reintroduce

00:49:39.170 --> 00:49:41.780 line:90%
the divine, say, into novels.

00:49:45.530 --> 00:49:47.370 line:90%
I like the way you put that.

00:49:47.370 --> 00:49:50.970 line:84%
Why aren't we just saying that
we're flattering ourselves

00:49:50.970 --> 00:49:52.870 line:90%
by reading these same things?

00:49:52.870 --> 00:49:55.560 line:84%
Part of my point,
and this is something

00:49:55.560 --> 00:49:59.310 line:84%
I wish I could have gotten into
a little bit more in the paper,

00:49:59.310 --> 00:50:03.000 line:84%
is that great
literature is always

00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:07.770 line:84%
taking those institutions that
we want to hold up as permanent

00:50:07.770 --> 00:50:14.820 line:84%
and unchanging and melting them
down, rebuilding them, seeing

00:50:14.820 --> 00:50:19.470 line:84%
how they can sustain the
kind of challenge that say,

00:50:19.470 --> 00:50:23.520 line:84%
Dante gives to the Church
by putting Popes in hell.

00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:24.496 line:90%
Right?

00:50:24.496 --> 00:50:30.630 line:84%
You're putting-- and then
having the Pope currently

00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:32.850 line:84%
in hell say, oh, I thought
you were the next one.

00:50:32.850 --> 00:50:36.830 line:90%
I was kind of waiting for him.

00:50:36.830 --> 00:50:40.560 line:84%
Dante is scathing in his
criticism of the Church.

00:50:40.560 --> 00:50:44.460 line:84%
But he's doing it in order to
try to restore the institution,

00:50:44.460 --> 00:50:49.560 line:84%
same thing with politics, right,
same thing with marriages.

00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:52.840 line:84%
We need to constantly
be thinking

00:50:52.840 --> 00:50:57.180 line:84%
how to renew the inner
being of these institutions,

00:50:57.180 --> 00:51:00.360 line:84%
just as we try to renew
the world around us

00:51:00.360 --> 00:51:02.243 line:90%
with metaphor and such things.

00:51:02.243 --> 00:51:03.410 line:90%
Thank you for that question.

00:51:06.630 --> 00:51:08.160 line:84%
AUDIENCE: Thank
you for your talk.

00:51:08.160 --> 00:51:09.820 line:84%
So I was wondering
what you thought

00:51:09.820 --> 00:51:13.270 line:84%
of film adaptations of
these great classical works,

00:51:13.270 --> 00:51:14.980 line:84%
like those of
Shakespeare or Homer.

00:51:14.980 --> 00:51:19.150 line:84%
I mean, obviously something is
lost going from page to screen

00:51:19.150 --> 00:51:21.100 line:84%
and they often take
some liberties.

00:51:21.100 --> 00:51:23.560 line:84%
But on the other
hand, it's a way

00:51:23.560 --> 00:51:25.540 line:84%
of exposing a more
casual audience who

00:51:25.540 --> 00:51:27.040 line:84%
wouldn't read those
books naturally

00:51:27.040 --> 00:51:28.730 line:90%
to those great stories.

00:51:28.730 --> 00:51:30.850 line:84%
So do you think there's
a value in that?

00:51:30.850 --> 00:51:32.800 line:90%
GLENN ARBERY: Absolutely.

00:51:32.800 --> 00:51:34.720 line:90%
In fact, I think--

00:51:34.720 --> 00:51:37.940 line:84%
I'll probably draw
criticism for this.

00:51:37.940 --> 00:51:44.290 line:84%
But I think we're in a great age
of a certain form of depiction.

00:51:44.290 --> 00:51:47.620 line:84%
It's more or less like
the serialized novel.

00:51:47.620 --> 00:51:52.900 line:84%
Some of the series, like
The Wire or Breaking Bad,

00:51:52.900 --> 00:51:54.130 line:90%
you raise your eyebrows.

00:51:54.130 --> 00:51:58.820 line:84%
But these are pretty
major accomplishments.

00:51:58.820 --> 00:52:03.640 line:84%
And I think when you can
serialize great books

00:52:03.640 --> 00:52:06.620 line:84%
in a way that doesn't
diminish, again,

00:52:06.620 --> 00:52:10.660 line:84%
the character of the world
that's being depicted in those,

00:52:10.660 --> 00:52:12.970 line:90%
I think it's a great service.

00:52:12.970 --> 00:52:17.350 line:84%
It might tell us that we're
back in an oral culture.

00:52:17.350 --> 00:52:18.850 line:90%
I don't know.

00:52:18.850 --> 00:52:25.450 line:84%
But it's not that
Shakespeare's works are written

00:52:25.450 --> 00:52:30.490 line:90%
to be handed out to readers.

00:52:30.490 --> 00:52:34.170 line:84%
They're presented as
works in the theater.

00:52:34.170 --> 00:52:36.160 line:84%
And there's a lot of
speculation about why

00:52:36.160 --> 00:52:38.080 line:84%
Shakespeare himself
never even bothered

00:52:38.080 --> 00:52:40.810 line:90%
to edit them for publication.

00:52:40.810 --> 00:52:46.630 line:84%
So that kind of presentation,
film, all these things, I think

00:52:46.630 --> 00:52:50.614 line:84%
is part of what gets
these works across.

00:52:50.614 --> 00:52:51.598 line:90%
Thank you.

00:52:55.050 --> 00:52:57.810 line:84%
AUDIENCE: Thank you so much for
coming to speak to us today.

00:52:57.810 --> 00:52:59.700 line:84%
A lot of modern
education will require

00:52:59.700 --> 00:53:01.825 line:84%
reading some of these
more challenging books

00:53:01.825 --> 00:53:03.450 line:84%
like The Scarlet
Letter and The Odyssey

00:53:03.450 --> 00:53:04.650 line:90%
and everything like that.

00:53:04.650 --> 00:53:06.025 line:84%
But a lot of
students will end up

00:53:06.025 --> 00:53:08.100 line:84%
just Spark Notesing it
or not even bothering

00:53:08.100 --> 00:53:09.580 line:90%
to read it at all.

00:53:09.580 --> 00:53:11.400 line:84%
And a lot of authors
are just coming up

00:53:11.400 --> 00:53:14.470 line:84%
with interesting plots that
don't ask the big questions.

00:53:14.470 --> 00:53:18.600 line:84%
So how would you say
we change the attitude

00:53:18.600 --> 00:53:21.000 line:84%
from trying to find the
instant gratification

00:53:21.000 --> 00:53:23.250 line:84%
and the entertaining
part of these stories,

00:53:23.250 --> 00:53:26.610 line:84%
to asking the bigger questions
and changing our perspectives?

00:53:26.610 --> 00:53:28.530 line:90%
GLENN ARBERY: Good question.

00:53:28.530 --> 00:53:32.540 line:84%
That's kind of "the"
question right now, isn't it?

00:53:32.540 --> 00:53:33.210 line:90%
I think you--

00:53:35.553 --> 00:53:37.470 line:84%
I'm suggesting it at the
end of my talk, well,

00:53:37.470 --> 00:53:39.680 line:84%
you just forbid
them to read them.

00:53:39.680 --> 00:53:42.300 line:90%
That'll do it.

00:53:42.300 --> 00:53:45.060 line:84%
You may not read
The Scarlet Letter.

00:53:45.060 --> 00:53:47.430 line:90%
You will never understand it.

00:53:47.430 --> 00:53:51.770 line:84%
It's too risque-- you
know, or whatever.

00:53:51.770 --> 00:53:57.910 line:84%
There's a way that I think
the real loss we face--

00:53:57.910 --> 00:54:01.770 line:84%
and I think this is in large
part because of the way

00:54:01.770 --> 00:54:06.450 line:84%
that we spend our time
on virtual media, cell

00:54:06.450 --> 00:54:08.400 line:90%
phones, and internet--

00:54:08.400 --> 00:54:12.760 line:84%
is just simply the loss of
the power of concentration.

00:54:12.760 --> 00:54:16.360 line:90%
So how to encourage that--

00:54:16.360 --> 00:54:19.420 line:84%
at Wyoming Catholic
we forbid cell phones.

00:54:19.420 --> 00:54:21.300 line:84%
You may not have a
cell phone while you're

00:54:21.300 --> 00:54:23.550 line:84%
a student at Wyoming
Catholic College.

00:54:23.550 --> 00:54:25.260 line:84%
In other words,
no, you don't get

00:54:25.260 --> 00:54:27.850 line:84%
to have it when you go
back to your dorm room.

00:54:27.850 --> 00:54:31.530 line:90%
You don't have one.

00:54:31.530 --> 00:54:33.700 line:84%
I mean, that's almost
like a gesture.

00:54:33.700 --> 00:54:38.430 line:84%
But it's a gesture in the
direction of concentration,

00:54:38.430 --> 00:54:41.400 line:84%
of the capacity
to take some time

00:54:41.400 --> 00:54:44.160 line:84%
and to sink into
something in a way that

00:54:44.160 --> 00:54:47.490 line:84%
allows you to experience
the language of it

00:54:47.490 --> 00:54:51.380 line:84%
and those deeper questions
that naturally arise from it.

00:54:51.380 --> 00:54:56.340 line:84%
It's also good to have people
around you in your classes

00:54:56.340 --> 00:54:58.800 line:84%
who care deeply
about these things

00:54:58.800 --> 00:55:03.060 line:84%
and push you so that
it's not something

00:55:03.060 --> 00:55:06.645 line:90%
that you can easily skate over.

00:55:06.645 --> 00:55:12.360 line:84%
Yeah, the Spark Notes
world of literature, that's

00:55:12.360 --> 00:55:14.250 line:90%
the great enemy.

00:55:14.250 --> 00:55:18.530 line:90%
Thank you

00:55:18.530 --> 00:55:20.390 line:90%
AUDIENCE: Thank you, Dr. Arbery.

00:55:20.390 --> 00:55:22.280 line:90%
I was just curious.

00:55:22.280 --> 00:55:25.160 line:84%
It seems like the modern
preoccupation with literature

00:55:25.160 --> 00:55:26.390 line:90%
is--

00:55:26.390 --> 00:55:28.910 line:84%
and the reason that they
dismiss the classics

00:55:28.910 --> 00:55:34.790 line:84%
is that everyone is
preoccupied with feelings.

00:55:34.790 --> 00:55:37.670 line:84%
And so the modern
authors, they try

00:55:37.670 --> 00:55:42.980 line:84%
to get us to try
and feel something

00:55:42.980 --> 00:55:47.030 line:84%
and not so much to
think about something.

00:55:47.030 --> 00:55:51.800 line:84%
How would you try to make the
argument to the modern thought

00:55:51.800 --> 00:55:55.460 line:84%
that where a well-formed
person should

00:55:55.460 --> 00:56:02.780 line:84%
cry at the end of
The Divine Comedy,

00:56:02.780 --> 00:56:05.450 line:90%
with the image of the divine?

00:56:05.450 --> 00:56:10.140 line:84%
That should be something
that you should have feelings

00:56:10.140 --> 00:56:12.410 line:90%
toward.

00:56:12.410 --> 00:56:14.990 line:84%
GLENN ARBERY: There's
a chapter in my book,

00:56:14.990 --> 00:56:19.200 line:84%
Why Literature Matters, called
the "Intelligence of Feeling."

00:56:19.200 --> 00:56:24.690 line:84%
And the premise of it is that
well-formed feelings contain

00:56:24.690 --> 00:56:28.920 line:84%
a great deal of experience and
a great deal of thought that's

00:56:28.920 --> 00:56:34.980 line:84%
gone into what people have
done in what you've read

00:56:34.980 --> 00:56:38.550 line:84%
and in what you've
experienced in your own life.

00:56:38.550 --> 00:56:44.640 line:84%
I look to feeling as a pretty
strong key to the truth

00:56:44.640 --> 00:56:48.580 line:90%
of things, if it's well formed.

00:56:48.580 --> 00:56:53.040 line:84%
So I don't want to
dismiss feeling.

00:56:53.040 --> 00:56:57.500 line:84%
On the other hand, this
appeal to sentiment,

00:56:57.500 --> 00:56:59.790 line:84%
I thought what Roger
Kimball said last night

00:56:59.790 --> 00:57:03.090 line:90%
about sentiment was dead on.

00:57:03.090 --> 00:57:10.080 line:84%
There's a kind of
sentimentality of fake emotion,

00:57:10.080 --> 00:57:15.600 line:84%
in a lot of what we see
now, that isn't earned,

00:57:15.600 --> 00:57:19.920 line:84%
it isn't part of
what would naturally

00:57:19.920 --> 00:57:24.540 line:84%
unfold from the actions and
circumstances of a work.

00:57:24.540 --> 00:57:29.970 line:84%
You appeal to a certain kind
of cultural trope or meme,

00:57:29.970 --> 00:57:34.200 line:84%
and that's supposed
to be sufficient.

00:57:34.200 --> 00:57:37.260 line:90%
It's a cue-- cry here.

00:57:37.260 --> 00:57:42.680 line:84%
Whereas the truly deep
works, like King Lear

00:57:42.680 --> 00:57:45.210 line:84%
when Cordelia comes
out in Lear's arms

00:57:45.210 --> 00:57:47.760 line:90%
at the end of that play--

00:57:47.760 --> 00:57:51.860 line:84%
If you're not feeling
anything there,

00:57:51.860 --> 00:57:54.120 line:90%
there's something deeply wrong.

00:57:54.120 --> 00:57:57.910 line:90%
So, great question, thank you.

00:57:57.910 --> 00:58:02.375 line:84%
EAMMON WEED: We now have
time for one more question.

00:58:02.375 --> 00:58:03.750 line:84%
AUDIENCE: Thank
you for speaking.

00:58:03.750 --> 00:58:07.620 line:84%
I have a question about-- you
mentioned that literature helps

00:58:07.620 --> 00:58:13.950 line:84%
form us and helps give us
the proper dispositions

00:58:13.950 --> 00:58:15.680 line:84%
that we should
have towards life,

00:58:15.680 --> 00:58:19.470 line:84%
and that classical
literature is useful

00:58:19.470 --> 00:58:24.340 line:84%
or takes a large part in
forming us in that way.

00:58:24.340 --> 00:58:27.840 line:84%
Do you think that modern,
or some modern literature,

00:58:27.840 --> 00:58:34.560 line:84%
can be a deformation or be
antithetical to that process?

00:58:34.560 --> 00:58:37.200 line:84%
And then, should we
avoid reading things

00:58:37.200 --> 00:58:41.070 line:84%
that do participate in
a deformation process?

00:58:41.070 --> 00:58:49.598 line:84%
And then how do we avoid
those malevolent works?

00:58:49.598 --> 00:58:51.390 line:84%
GLENN ARBERY: Again,
an important question.

00:58:51.390 --> 00:58:55.440 line:84%
I think it's hard to
know in advance what

00:58:55.440 --> 00:58:58.330 line:90%
a work is going to do to you.

00:58:58.330 --> 00:58:59.760 line:90%
I worry about this.

00:58:59.760 --> 00:59:02.985 line:84%
I worry about students
who have moral qualms,

00:59:02.985 --> 00:59:05.820 line:84%
say, about reading
Madame Bovary.

00:59:05.820 --> 00:59:10.800 line:84%
They're scared they're going
to be tempted in some way.

00:59:10.800 --> 00:59:16.170 line:84%
You're going to be tempted
toward much worse literature

00:59:16.170 --> 00:59:17.590 line:90%
if you don't read it.

00:59:17.590 --> 00:59:24.120 line:84%
I think that it's
difficult to judge

00:59:24.120 --> 00:59:29.580 line:84%
what's deforming if you
haven't been formed, right?

00:59:29.580 --> 00:59:32.040 line:84%
And if you have
been formed, I don't

00:59:32.040 --> 00:59:35.820 line:84%
think that the
danger of deformation

00:59:35.820 --> 00:59:39.460 line:90%
is going to be very powerful.

00:59:39.460 --> 00:59:42.210 line:84%
So, I mean, we know certain
things are deforming.

00:59:42.210 --> 00:59:45.750 line:90%
Pornography is deforming, right?

00:59:45.750 --> 00:59:53.520 line:84%
But an honest attempt to give a
kind of vision of the world we

00:59:53.520 --> 00:59:59.460 line:84%
live in through literature
doesn't seem to me deforming,

00:59:59.460 --> 01:00:04.230 line:84%
unless we are conscious from
the outset that as Keats says,

01:00:04.230 --> 01:00:07.590 line:84%
it has a design
on you; that it's

01:00:07.590 --> 01:00:09.690 line:84%
trying to get you to
do something or think

01:00:09.690 --> 01:00:11.190 line:90%
a certain way.

01:00:11.190 --> 01:00:17.130 line:84%
If it's an honest attempt to
depict the world that we live

01:00:17.130 --> 01:00:21.720 line:84%
in and the characters
who live in it,

01:00:21.720 --> 01:00:25.230 line:84%
I think you have to
risk a little bit.

01:00:25.230 --> 01:00:27.452 line:84%
Good question,
though, thank you.

01:00:27.452 --> 01:00:28.540 line:90%
And thank you all.

01:00:28.540 --> 01:00:28.825 line:90%
I appreciate it.

01:00:28.825 --> 01:00:30.283 line:84%
EAMMON WEED: Thank
you, Dr. Arbery.

01:00:30.283 --> 01:00:32.650 line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

